// spec: VAR-006
//
// A value whose type could not be determined does not satisfy a written type,
// at an ASSIGNMENT exactly as at a declaration.
//
// ⚠️ The assignment used to permit it while the declaration refused it -- the
// same value reaching the same variable one line later, with nothing checking
// it.  That asymmetry was forced by weak inference: refusing an untyped value
// at an assignment refused correct code, starting with the compiler's own
// sources.  Resolving it wanted better inference, not a looser rule.
//
// ⚠️ Writing no type stays entirely permissive.  The rule bites only where a
// type was written down, and 'as' is how a value earns the crossing.

function Untyped (); begin Exit 'xy'; end

var S : String;
S := Untyped ();
